# Activity titles, student-facing directions, and what to collect next.
STUDENT = [
 [
 ("Pip looks closely","Look at a photograph of your class plant. Point to one leaf. Draw the part you can see.","I can see…"),
 ("Milla spots a change","Look at a second picture. Draw the two pictures side by side. Tell a partner one thing that looks different. With one picture, draw it again from a new angle and show the difference in view.","In the first picture… / In the next picture…"),
 ("Dot asks a question","What would you like to see next? Draw it. Ask your teacher to write your question if you like.","My question is…")
 ],
 [
 ("Read a coffee leaf","Pick out two features on the same dated photo. Sketch and label the leaf edge, stem or tip that you can see.","The date and source are…"),
 ("Make two observation cards","Use one box for each date. Draw and caption each view. Mark a feature you can compare in both. Leave the second date empty if you have one entry.","One visible change is…"),
 ("Ask the farmer","Tell a partner what you saw and one thing you wonder. Write a class question with a drawing.","We observed… / We wonder…")
 ],
 [
 ("Draw with labels","Choose a dated farmer entry. Sketch one visible plant feature. Add a label describing its shape or colour. Record where the information came from.","My drawing shows…"),
 ("Make a measurement record","Record the date, height in centimetres, and method if the farmer published them. Keep unknown cells blank. With teacher guidance, practise using the soil line and highest leaf on the same classroom model each time.","Label readings from your model as classroom practice."),
 ("Show a pattern","Plot comparable height readings on the graph. Put elapsed days on the lower axis and centimetres on the side. With just one farm reading, plot one point and plan a future observation.","I would record ______ next time because ______.")
 ],
 [
 ("What could affect growth?","Read the field note and the weather source. List two conditions that could affect the plant. Record an observation separately from your explanation.","Evidence from the tree… / An idea I would test…"),
 ("Design a fair comparison","Choose light or water as your one changed condition on classroom seedlings. Keep seed type, container, growing medium, start date and measurement method the same where possible. Ask your teacher to check the plan.","What will change? What will stay the same?"),
 ("Review your evidence","Plot compatible readings or use clearly labelled classroom data. Ask whether the trend fits your prediction. Explain another factor that could affect the pattern.","My conclusion and one limit…")
 ],
 [
 ("Build an evidence table","Use the farmer’s notes to record dates, height if published, measurement method and source. Check whether each reading comes from the same planting and the same technique.","Which entries are comparable and why?"),
 ("Graph compatible readings","Plot height against elapsed days. Mark missing values separately and justify your axes. Where farm data are sparse, use clearly labelled classroom-model data and explain the choice.","Show date range, units and one pattern."),
 ("Interpret the orchard record","Compare your graph with dated rainfall if available. Identify what the records support and one possible confounding factor, such as watering, temperature or measurement technique.","What would you collect to strengthen your explanation?")
 ],
 [
 ("Test a claim","Choose a specific claim about growth from the class journal. Write it as a question you can examine. Identify the evidence you would need and the time period.","Make the claim specific to the current planting."),
 ("Examine reliability","Select compatible records or design classroom repeats. Show uncertainty and consider sample size and variation. Keep field and model results in separate series.","What would make the measurements more repeatable?"),
 ("Write an evidence-based brief","Write a claim, give a dated source, explain the connection, and state one limitation. Describe one observation that might challenge your explanation.","What finding would make me change my conclusion?")
 ]
]
